    },
    {
      "id": "xuJH47btNY_j6ZhoWYuyH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4797.18825194441,
      "y": 823.2994407437504,
      "width": 467.8943775504262,
      "height": 223.67006863347245,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b7v",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1958409184,
      "version": 1441,
      "versionNonce": 1030834144,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789642043654,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Exemplo:\n\nImaginamos que temos o serviço de PaymentProcessor que fica responsável dentro do\nsistema por iniciar o processamento do pagamento, mas não conhece detalhes\nespecíficos de cartão de crédito, Pix, PayPal ou boleto. Ele depende apenas do\ncontrato PaymentMethod.\n\nCada forma de pagamento implementa a interface PaymentMethod e fornece sua\nprópria lógica no método pay().\n\nQuando for necessário adicionar um novo meio de pagamento, como boleto,\ncriptomoeda ou carteira digital, basta criar uma nova classe que implemente essa\ninterface — sem alterar a classe PaymentProcessor.\n\nAssim, o sistema fica aberto para extensão e fechado para modificação, atendendo\nao princípio OCP do SOLID.",
      "fontSize": 11.183503431673623,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Exemplo:\n\nImaginamos que temos o serviço de PaymentProcessor que fica responsável dentro do sistema por iniciar o processamento do pagamento, mas não conhece detalhes específicos de cartão de crédito, Pix, PayPal ou boleto. Ele depende apenas do contrato PaymentMethod.\n\nCada forma de pagamento implementa a interface PaymentMethod e fornece sua própria lógica no método pay().\n\nQuando for necessário adicionar um novo meio de pagamento, como boleto, criptomoeda ou carteira digital, basta criar uma nova classe que implemente essa interface — sem alterar a classe PaymentProcessor.\n\nAssim, o sistema fica aberto para extensão e fechado para modificação, atendendo ao princípio OCP do SOLID.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7RmxuqVWUIx9cWwJjJ5DE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5415.118629717591,
      "y": 823.8521678607076,
      "width": 429.6664149821925,
      "height": 279.5875857918406,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b80",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 168200160,
      "version": 1437,
      "versionNonce": 857161248,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789643180709,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Classes should be open for extension but closed for modification.\n\nThe main idea behind this principle is to prevent existing code from breaking\nwhen new functionality is introduced.\n\nA class should allow its base behavior to be extended, enabling you to create\nsubclasses and override only the parts of the original class that need to\nbehave differently. This helps ensure that existing client code continues to\nwork as expected.\n\nHowever, this principle should not be applied blindly in every scenario. It was\nnot designed to solve every problem. If you find a bug in a class, simply fix\nit—do not create a subclass just to work around it. As Alexander Shvets\nstates in his book: “A child class should not be responsible for the problems\nof its parent class.”\n\nIn my view, this principle is directly related to the previous one. If you have a\nwell-designed class with a clear, cohesive, and well-defined responsibility, it\ndoes not make sense to introduce additional complexity that falls outside the\nclass’s domain.",
      "fontSize": 11.183503431673623,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Classes should be open for extension but closed for modification.\n\nThe main idea behind this principle is to prevent existing code from breaking when new functionality is introduced.\n\nA class should allow its base behavior to be extended, enabling you to create subclasses and override only the parts of the original class that need to behave differently. This helps ensure that existing client code continues to work as expected.\n\nHowever, this principle should not be applied blindly in every scenario. It was not designed to solve every problem. If you find a bug in a class, simply fix it—do not create a subclass just to work around it. As Alexander Shvets states in his book: “A child class should not be responsible for the problems of its parent class.”\n\nIn my view, this principle is directly related to the previous one. If you have a well-designed class with a clear, cohesive, and well-defined responsibility, it does not make sense to introduce additional complexity that falls outside the class’s domain.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XxSrOFizbzjy3tKg3DQiM",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 5382.54865199529,
      "y": 757.6143385714458,
      "width": 1005.4545454545455,
      "height": 440.9090909090909,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b81",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1001032672,
      "version": 471,
      "versionNonce": 1495683616,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789639807469,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "RMhGeu5q_UfljiOeEWwDV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5415.118629086199,
      "y": 774.259766573161,
      "width": 309.23980712890625,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#868e96",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b82",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 573444064,
      "version": 294,
      "versionNonce": 550964768,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789639807469,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "My understood about it (en-us)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "My understood about it (en-us)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "sN6jL8AGUmhANjtI6IWAt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5885.275925502044,
      "y": 822.2910533212848,
      "width": 467.8943775504262,
      "height": 223.67006863347245,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b83",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1973490656,
      "version": 1370,
      "versionNonce": 1761029664,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789643187364,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Example:\n\nLet’s imagine we have a PaymentProcessor service responsible for initiating payment\nprocessing within the system. However, it does not need to know the implementation\ndetails of specific payment methods such as credit cards, Pix, PayPal, or bank slips.\nInstead, it depends only on the PaymentMethod contract.\n\nEach payment method implements the PaymentMethod interface and provides its own\npayment logic through the pay() method.\n\nWhenever a new payment method needs to be supported—such as a bank slip,\ncryptocurrency, or a digital wallet—you only need to create a new class that\nimplements this interface. There is no need to modify the PaymentProcessor class.\n\nAs a result, the system remains open for extension and closed for modification, which\nfollows the SOLID Open/Closed Principle (OCP).",
      "fontSize": 11.183503431673623,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Example:\n\nLet’s imagine we have a PaymentProcessor service responsible for initiating payment processing within the system. However, it does not need to know the implementation details of specific payment methods such as credit cards, Pix, PayPal, or bank slips. Instead, it depends only on the PaymentMethod contract.\n\nEach payment method implements the PaymentMethod interface and provides its own payment logic through the pay() method.\n\nWhenever a new payment method needs to be supported—such as a bank slip, cryptocurrency, or a digital wallet—you only need to create a new class that implements this interface. There is no need to modify the PaymentProcessor class.\n\nAs a result, the system remains open for extension and closed for modification, which follows the SOLID Open/Closed Principle (OCP).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
