    },
    {
      "id": "-C13apr3qjVgRewMBKLK-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 9867.196197146133,
      "y": 774.2597665243634,
      "width": 309.23980712890625,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#868e96",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1387542048,
      "version": 395,
      "versionNonce": 1085789152,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789647160413,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "My understood about it (en-us)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "My understood about it (en-us)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FWnLrIzxcv_2t-xV2jVEh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10337.35349356198,
      "y": 822.2910532724873,
      "width": 467.8943775504262,
      "height": 265.60820650224855,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1997042208,
      "version": 1472,
      "versionNonce": 1353002528,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789647858036,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In practical terms: if a subclass needs to throw an\n`UnsupportedOperationException`, ignore inherited methods, or alter expected\nresults, the hierarchy is likely poorly modeled.\n\nThis is the example I will discuss later on!\n\nBank account\n\nThis example is quite intuitive: a standard account allows withdrawals, whereas an\ninvestment account might not permit immediate withdrawal. Making\n`InvestmentAccount` inherit from an account whose contract promises withdrawal\ncapabilities violates the LSP.\n\nThe solution is to separate contracts based on capabilities: every account can check\nits balance, but only withdrawable accounts should implement the withdrawal\noperation.\n\nAn investment account cannot even be accidentally passed to that service, as it\ndoes not implement `WithdrawableAccount`.",
      "fontSize": 11.183503431673623,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In practical terms: if a subclass needs to throw an `UnsupportedOperationException`, ignore inherited methods, or alter expected results, the hierarchy is likely poorly modeled.\n\nThis is the example I will discuss later on!\n\nBank account\n\nThis example is quite intuitive: a standard account allows withdrawals, whereas an investment account might not permit immediate withdrawal. Making `InvestmentAccount` inherit from an account whose contract promises withdrawal capabilities violates the LSP.\n\nThe solution is to separate contracts based on capabilities: every account can check its balance, but only withdrawable accounts should implement the withdrawal operation.\n\nAn investment account cannot even be accidentally passed to that service, as it does not implement `WithdrawableAccount`.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
